I'm joined today by Abigail Marsh who's  an associate professor of psychology and  neuroscience at Georgetown University  also author of the book The Fear Factor  how one emotion connects altruist  Psychopaths and everyone in-between so  Abigail to get us into this topic let's  first sort of try to get everybody on  the same page in terms of when we say  psychopathy and that someone is a  psychopath what are we talking about I'm  glad you asked that because there's a  ton of misconceptions about psychopathy  um it's not an official clinical  diagnosis it's considered a personality  disorder and it's a constellation of a  number of personality traits that hang  together one of which is in the sort of  signature one of which is a kind of  callous uncaring personality and a  tendency not to experience remorse after  having done something wrong and you also  have traits that also tend to go with  that like manipulative nests  deceitfulness and sometimes  impulsiveness as well and when we talk I  mean and you can answer this generally  about personality disorders or more  specifically about psychopathy how much  do we consider or how much do we know  about how much nature versus nurture  play into these personality disorders  it's a really important question the  estimate for how heritable psychopathy  is comes from quite a few studies now  usually on twins who are either  identical or fraternal and those studies  suggest that variation and psychopathic  traits is about sixty percent due to  genetic factors with the rest being due  to sort of idiosyncratic environmental  factors and does that mean in in late  I'm sorry in layman's terms could that  mean that one might have a  predisposition to psychopathy that might  or might not manifest depending on  environmental conditions absolutely  that's exactly what it means so it means  that things that you inherit from your  parents you know temperamental traits  for example fearlessness there's a very  good study recently showing that the  fearlessness of the biological mother  predicts fearlessness and the biological  child which then  goes on to predict callous and uncaring  traits later on but that those traits  can absolutely be mitigated or affected  by all sorts of other things that happen  for example how warm and caring the  mother is so what are the sort of  activities or what we might consider  flags of possible psychopathic  tendencies in kids and what's the  earliest point at which those might be  observed we think that there might be  markers of psychopathic traits that can  be reliably detected as early as maybe 2  or 3 one of the things I study is how  sensitive people are to others fear and  we know that children who are unusually  callous and uncaring already are showing  weaknesses and identifying others fear  by age 2 or 3 and if we're all sort of  fearless temperament coupled with sort  of a callous responsiveness to other  people's distress I tend to be the early  risk factors for psychopathy luckily  things like temper tantrums hitting  people when you're angry those are not  early signs of psychopathy those are for  better for words just normal toddler  behaviors yeah that's interesting I mean  in your work do you sometimes have  parents who are worried that their child  might be exhibiting psychopathic  tendencies who are just not and it  causes significant concern for the  parents oh absolutely you know parents  will you know discover that their child  is biting other children at preschool or  having this horrible temper tantrums and  or terrified that their child is some  sort of a monster but the you know the  reality is that most two and  three-year-olds are sort of monsters at  least some of the time and you know for  the most part they all learn to control  their behavior later on the only  children that you really want to worry  about are children who seem to sort of  and generally fearless across the board  and and it's easy to confuse  fearlessness with boldness so for  example my child loved to climb high  structures to explore them when she was  younger and people would say was she so  fearless and of course I said no she's  nobody because I've seen her in the dark  and I've seen her in the water and she's  actually quite fearful in other  circumstances but she did love to  explore and so that was why she would  climb on these  climber's the bigger concern is children  who are fearless across contacts they  don't seem to be afraid of anything I've  worked with adolescents who when I asked  them to report the last time they felt  really frightens told me that they never  felt friend of anything and that's  that's pretty unusual so as early as two  or three you can start to see some of  these tendencies when we're talking  about a 5 7 or 9 year old in in your  experience what are some of the  behaviors that you might start to see so  that's a tricky age because most of the  instruments that are used for assessing  callous and unemotional traits as  they're called in children are not  really designed for assessing children  not young because some of Eric turistic  behaviors that you'll you'll see later  on haven't really started developing  quite so young so admittedly it's a  really difficult age to pin anything  down for sure but again what I would be  looking for is again it's sort of  generally fearless temperament sort of  insensitivity to any kind of threat and  that also means insensitivity of  punishment this is one of the reasons  that psychopathy is associated with so  many problems is because in normal  children and in most people if you  believe that you're going to be punished  for doing something fear of getting  punished is what stops you from doing it  not every time maybe depending on how  fun that thing seems like it might be  but in general it has some effect on you  but if you don't experience fear  strongly you're not going to be worried  at all about possible punishments for  what you're doing and so Truman pervy  assess to punishment is definitely  troubling for a number of reasons is  there some generally accepted set of  questions that could be sort of a test  for psychopathy in the way that you have  you know the six questions to figure out  if someone might be depressed for  example or something along those lines  well so the newest version of the  Diagnostic and Statistical Manual in  psychiatry has included this little four  item specifier that is used to assess  again what are called callous  unemotional traits or  pro-social emotions in children who are  already having significant behavior  problems so among children who are  having significant behavior problems and  there are a lot of reasons that could be  many children who act out and are  aggressive and disruptive did not have  these traits but the indicators for the  ones that do include things like not  showing empathy or remorse not seeming  to care about outcomes like how well you  do in school or how your friendships are  going and having sort of flat effect  although again in general it seems to be  most relevant whether the child is  fearful or fearless so I instinctively  mitigate the possible negative  repercussions of psychopathy or what  what can be done it's it's true that  when it comes to personality disorders  you're not looking for a sort of a whole  overhaul of person's personality but um  certainly there are ways that you can  work with children to help them develop  and more adaptive ways there haven't  been that many really stringently tested  methods for improving in particular  these sort of callous and uncaring  traits and children  there aren't any particular  pharmaceuticals that are developed for  it although many children with these  traits are treated with pharmaceuticals  used for other disorders so for example  many of these children get stimulants  that are used for ADHD some of them  getting into psychotics that are used  for obviously psychosis and sometimes  