le who  were strongly psychopathic is that they  also tend to be fairly narcissistic  meaning they tend to think they're great  I've worked with children with these  traits before and one of the questions  that we've asked them in the past is to  just assess themselves on a scale from  one to ten with ten being I feel amazing  about myself and one being I feel  terrible about myself and at a typical  kid when you ask them to answer that  question will respond with like a seven  or an eight  and again we're screening out children  who have depression or anxiety who might  give a lower score on any case when we  asked children who have psychopathic  traits to answer the same question and  again these are kids who  you know don't have any real friends  have been thrown out of multiple schools  their parents are afraid of them  you know objectively speaking their life  is not going that well but they'll tend  to answer this question with you know 10  Miley's I was even like 11 or 20 and  that's a big problem because if you want  somebody to change them thinking that  they're as great as they could possibly  be is not a great starting point and so  just getting people motivated to change  is a really difficult stumbling block  unfortunately how common is the often  sort of caricature or stereotype of the  psychopathic kid of harming animals how  common is that in reality uh yeah that  is that definitely corresponds to risk  for psychopathy absolutely it sort of  depends on the age because in younger  children there may not be real  understanding you know if you think of  like a very small child you know burning  ants with a magnifying glass or  something I mean you could argue that  they may not really understand what it  is that they're doing but mistreating  animals you know pass through early very  early school age especially animals that  can obviously signify that they're in  pain or that they are suffering is  definitely a really serious problem the  book is the fear factor how one emotion  connects altruist psychopaths and  everyone in between we've been speaking  with the book's author Abigail Marsh  thank you so much for talking to us  about this thanks very much for having  me

